var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#AnnotatedTests.jl","page":"Home","text":"AnnotatedTests.jl adds named, annotated tests to Julia's standard testing workflow. It is intended for teaching contexts where a failing test should give a student a concrete next step, not only a Boolean failure.\n\n@annotated_test \"absolute value\" abs(x) >= 0 ctx -> \"The absolute value should never be negative.\"","title":"AnnotatedTests.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"using AnnotatedTests\n\n@annotated_test \"sorts a vector\" mysort([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3] \\\n    \"Return the values in increasing order.\"\n\n@atest \"same values\" sort(student_values()) == [1, 2, 3] unordered_feedback()\n\nUse @annotated_testset when you want a pedagogical name for a group of related checks:\n\n@annotated_testset \"Assignment 1\" begin\n    @annotated_test \"question 1\" q1() == 42 \"Review the formula for q1.\"\nend\n\nThe feedback handler may be a string, a function, or a callable object. Function handlers receive an AnnotationContext, which includes evaluated left and right values for supported binary comparisons. The traditional ctx.lhs and ctx.rhs names are available alongside teaching-oriented aliases: ctx.observed, ctx.expected, ctx.LHS, and ctx.RHS.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Supported-Comparisons","page":"Home","text":"AnnotatedTests captures the left and right side of ==, !=, <, <=, >, >=, ===, !==, in, ∈, isa, and ≈. Each side is evaluated once.\n\nFor non-binary expressions, the context still records the test name and original expression, but the binary-expression fields are nothing.\n\n≈ also records ctx.difference when a difference can be computed.","title":"Supported Comparisons"},{"category":"section","location":"#Feedback-Helpers","page":"Home","text":"@atest \"close answer\" estimate() ≈ 10 compare_feedback(message=\"Check your rounding.\")\n@atest \"close answer with tolerance\" estimate() ≈ 10 atol=0.01\n@atest \"right type\" answer() isa Vector{Int} type_feedback()\n@atest \"right length\" answer() == expected length_feedback()\n@atest \"same items\" answer() == expected unordered_feedback()\n\nTrailing keyword arguments other than broken and skip are forwarded to the tested function or comparison, matching Test.@test:\n\n@annotated_test \"pi approximation\" π ≈ 3.14 atol=0.01\n@annotated_test \"pi approximation\" isapprox(π, 3.14) atol=0.01","title":"Feedback Helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Broken-And-Skipped-Tests","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_test \"stretch goal\" advanced_answer() == expected \\\n    \"This check documents the next feature to implement.\" broken=true\n\n@atest \"requires optional data\" answer_from_file() == expected skip=!isfile(path)\n\nWhen broken or skip evaluates to true, the test expression is not evaluated. This mirrors Julia's Test.@test behavior.","title":"Broken And Skipped Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"#Exception-Tests","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_test_throws \"rejects empty input\" ArgumentError parse_answer(\"\") \\\n    \"Empty input should throw an ArgumentError.\"\n\n@atest_throws \"rejects missing file\" SystemError read(\"missing.txt\")\n\nThe expected exception can be a type, tuple of types, exception value, string, or regular expression. Feedback handlers receive ctx.thrown, ctx.thrown_type, ctx.expected_exception, and ctx.message.","title":"Exception Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"#Custom-Operators","page":"Home","text":"Use register_annotated_operator! to add a relation and optional terms:\n\nrelapprox(x, y; rtol=0.05) = abs(x - y) / max(abs(y), eps()) <= rtol\n\nregister_annotated_operator!(:relapprox; terms=(lhs, rhs) -> (\n    relative_difference = abs(lhs - rhs) / max(abs(rhs), eps()),\n))\n\n@atest \"relative error\" relapprox(student_answer(), 100; rtol=0.02) ctx ->\n    \"The relative difference was $(ctx.relative_difference); it must be below 2%.\"","title":"Custom Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Home","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@annotated_test","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_test name expr [on_error] [test_keyword=value...] [broken=condition] [skip=condition]\n@atest name expr [on_error] [test_keyword=value...] [broken=condition] [skip=condition]\n\nRun a test like Test.@test, but attach teacher-written feedback when it fails. The feedback handler may be a string, function, or callable object. Functions receive an AnnotationContext.\n\nThe macro evaluates each side of a supported binary comparison exactly once, so expressions with side effects such as pop!(xs) == 3 are safe to annotate.\n\nExamples\n\n@annotated_test \"sorted output\" mysort([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3] \\\n    \"Return the values in increasing order.\"\n\n@atest \"close enough\" estimate() ≈ 10 ctx ->\n    \"Difference was $(ctx.difference).\"\n\n@atest \"within tolerance\" estimate() ≈ 10 atol=0.01\n\n@annotated_test \"documented issue\" current_answer() == expected broken=true\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@annotated_test"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@atest","page":"Home","text":"@atest name expr [on_error] [test_keyword=value...] [broken=condition] [skip=condition]\n\nShort alias for @annotated_test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@atest"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@annotated_test_throws","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_test_throws name expected_exception expr [on_error] [broken=condition] [skip=condition]\n@atest_throws name expected_exception expr [on_error] [broken=condition] [skip=condition]\n\nRun a test like Test.@test_throws, but attach teacher-written feedback when it fails. The expected exception may be an exception type, tuple of exception types, exception value, string, or regular expression.\n\nFeedback handlers receive an AnnotationContext. For throwing tests, ctx.expected is the expected exception specification, ctx.observed is the thrown exception or nothing, and terms include ctx.thrown, ctx.thrown_type, ctx.expected_exception, and ctx.message.\n\nExample\n\n@annotated_test_throws \"rejects empty input\" ArgumentError parse_answer(\"\") \\\n    \"Empty input should throw an ArgumentError.\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@annotated_test_throws"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@atest_throws","page":"Home","text":"@atest_throws name expected_exception expr [on_error] [broken=condition] [skip=condition]\n\nShort alias for @annotated_test_throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@atest_throws"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@annotated_testset","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_testset name begin ... end\n\nA light wrapper over Test.@testset for pedagogical test groups.\n\nExample\n\n@annotated_testset \"Assignment 1\" begin\n    @annotated_test \"question 1\" q1() == 42 \"Review the formula for q1.\"\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@annotated_testset"},{"category":"type","location":"#AnnotatedTests.AnnotationContext","page":"Home","text":"AnnotationContext\n\nStructured information made available to feedback handlers when an annotated test fails.\n\nFields include the test name, the original test expression expr, the detected binary operator op when there is one, the unevaluated left and right expressions, their evaluated values, the final Boolean value, and any operator-specific terms.\n\nFor non-binary expressions, op, lhs_expr, rhs_expr, lhs, and rhs are nothing.\n\nAliases are available for common teaching language:\n\nctx.observed and ctx.LHS are aliases for ctx.lhs\nctx.expected and ctx.RHS are aliases for ctx.rhs\noperator-specific terms such as ctx.difference are read from ctx.terms\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.AnnotationContext"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.default_feedback","page":"Home","text":"default_feedback(ctx::AnnotationContext)\n\nDefault human-readable feedback message for a failed annotated test.\n\nThis function is used when @annotated_test is called without an explicit feedback handler. It reports the test name, expression, observed and expected values for supported binary comparisons, and operator-specific terms such as difference when available.\n\nExample\n\n@annotated_test \"sorted output\" student_sort([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3]\n\nExample output:\n\nTest: sorted output\nExpression: :(student_sort([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3])\nObserved: [3, 1, 2]\nExpected: [1, 2, 3]\nOperator: ==\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.default_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.compare_feedback","page":"Home","text":"compare_feedback(; message=nothing, observed_label=\"Observed\", expected_label=\"Expected\")\n\nCreate a feedback handler for ordinary comparisons. The returned handler prints the original expression, observed value, expected value, and any operator terms.\n\nExamples\n\n@annotated_test \"computed value\" student_answer() == expected compare_feedback()\n\n@annotated_test \"computed value\" student_answer() == expected \\\n    compare_feedback(message=\"Check the formula used to compute this value.\")\n\n@annotated_test \"computed value\" student_answer() == expected \\\n    compare_feedback(observed_label=\"Returned\", expected_label=\"Target\")\n\nExample output:\n\nExpression: :(student_answer() == expected)\nReturned: 17\nTarget: 42\nCheck the formula used to compute this value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.compare_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.type_feedback","page":"Home","text":"type_feedback([expected])\n\nCreate feedback for type checks. If expected is omitted and the annotated expression uses isa, the right-hand side of the comparison is shown.\n\nExamples\n\n@annotated_test \"returns integers\" student_values() isa Vector{Int} type_feedback()\n\n@annotated_test \"returns vector\" student_values() isa AbstractVector \\\n    type_feedback(Vector{Int})\n\nExample output:\n\nExpected type: Vector{Int64}\nObserved type: Vector{Float64}\nObserved value: [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.type_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.length_feedback","page":"Home","text":"length_feedback([expected])\n\nCreate feedback that includes observed and expected lengths when they are available.\n\nExamples\n\n@annotated_test \"preserves length\" student_values() == expected_values length_feedback()\n\n@annotated_test \"returns three values\" student_values() == expected_values \\\n    length_feedback(3)\n\nExample output:\n\nObserved length: 2\nExpected length: 3\nObserved value: [1, 2]\nExpected value: [1, 2, 3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.length_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.unordered_feedback","page":"Home","text":"unordered_feedback()\n\nCreate feedback for collection comparisons where order might be the issue.\n\nExample\n\n@annotated_test \"same values\" student_values() == [1, 2, 3] unordered_feedback()\n\nExample output:\n\nThe values match, but the order is different.\nObserved: [1, 3, 2]\nExpected: [1, 2, 3]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.unordered_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.register_annotated_operator!","page":"Home","text":"register_annotated_operator!(op::Symbol; terms=(lhs, rhs) -> (;))\n\nRegister an operator or binary relation that @annotated_test should treat as a comparison. The optional terms function receives the evaluated left and right values and must return a NamedTuple.\n\nThose terms become available in feedback handlers as ctx.terms and as direct properties. For example, a term named difference can be read as ctx.difference.\n\nExample\n\nrelapprox(x, y; rtol=0.05) = abs(x - y) / max(abs(y), eps()) <= rtol\n\nregister_annotated_operator!(:relapprox; terms=(lhs, rhs) -> (\n    relative_difference = abs(lhs - rhs) / max(abs(rhs), eps()),\n))\n\n@atest \"relative error\" relapprox(student_answer(), 100; rtol=0.02) ctx ->\n    \"The relative difference was $(ctx.relative_difference); it must be below 2%.\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.register_annotated_operator!"}]
}
